is there a memory leak in this code spinnet?```cpp
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    char* str = new char[40];
    str = "salam peyman";
    int index = 0;
    for (auto ch = str[0]; ch != '\0'; ch = str[++index] ) {
        std::cout << ch;
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

- **Reasoning:** The code allocates memory using `new` but never deallocates it with `delete`. This leads to a memory leak.
- **Solution:**
  ```cpp
  #include <iostream>
  int main() {
      char* str = new char[40];
      strcpy(str, "salam peyman"); // Use strcpy instead of direct assignment
      int index = 0;
      for (auto ch = str[0]; ch != '\0'; ch = str[++index] ) {
          std::cout << ch;
      }
      std::cout << std::endl;
      delete[] str; // Free the allocated memory
      return 0;
  }
  ```
- **Justification:** `strcpy` is used to safely copy the string into the allocated memory. The memory is then properly freed using `delete[]` to prevent a memory leak.

when i put 'delete[] str' in the code, the program crashes.```cpp
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    char* str = new char[40];
    str = "salam peyman";
    int index = 0;
    for (auto ch = str[0]; ch != '\0'; ch = str[++index] ) {
        std::cout << ch;
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    delete [] str;
    return 0;
}
```

- **Reasoning:** The program crashes because the string assigned to `str` is a literal, and literals are stored in read-only memory. Attempting to modify or deallocate them leads to undefined behavior.
- **Solution:**
  ```cpp
  #include <iostream>
  int main() {
      char str[40] = "salam peyman"; // Allocate array on stack
      int index = 0;
      for (auto ch = str[0]; ch != '\0'; ch = str[++index]) {
          std::cout << ch;
      }
      std::cout << std::endl;
      return 0;
  }
  ```
- **Justification:** By declaring `str` as an array on the stack, it avoids the issue of trying to deallocate a read-only literal. The program will run without crashing and correctly print "salam peyman".
